<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Khongirad</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Hongirad, Onggirat, Qongirat, Khongirat, Khunggirad, Qoñyrat</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Khongirad were one of the major divisions of the Mongol tribes, with a homeland near Lake Hulun in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inner-mongolia">Inner Mongolia</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khalkha-mongols">Khalkha</a> River in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongolia">Mongolia</a>. Their clans never united under a single leader, and they are known above all as the chief consort clan of the house of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genghis-khan">Genghis Khan</a>: his mother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hoelun">Hoelun</a>, his great-grandmother and his first wife were Khongirads, as were the wives of most rulers of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuan-dynasty">Yuan dynasty</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-horde">Golden Horde</a>. Their power at the Yuan court peaked under Dagi and Temüder in the reign of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gegeen-khan">Gegeen Khan</a>. In the west, Khongirad elites founded the Sufi dynasty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khwarazm">Khwarazm</a> (1359–1388), and later Qongirat inaks ruled the Khanate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khiva">Khiva</a>, whose last khan abdicated in 1920.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/khongirad.jpg" alt="Khongirad" width="330" height="216" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Khiruge · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Khongirads_map.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
